    "created_at": "2022-07-04T15:46:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-07-06T09:54:36Z",
    "body": "> It would indeed be:\r\n> \r\n> 1. preconnect / preload can also be specified in the headers, possibly even with 103 Early Hint\r\n\r\nTrue, though the headers and the first part of the response body could be in the same TCP packet and so the difference in timing would be marginal (in the 200 OK case).\r\n\r\n> 2. I think that the remainder of the main thread execution delays the connection to the socket (if not according to the spec, then by browser implementations)\r\n\r\nHmm. https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-websocket-establish says to return after parsing the URL, and establishing a websocket connection \"in parallel\" (which typically means in a separate thread).\r\n\r\nhttps://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#section-4.1 step 2 says to wait if there are any connections to the same IP until they're either opened or closed. So if you try to open multiple connections at once, the browser will serialize them so that only one is attempting a connection at a time.\r\n\r\n[In #whatwg Matrix I discussed](https://matrixlogs.bakkot.com/WHATWG/2022-07-04) with @yutakahirano whether there's any delay from the script running in the main thread on opening the network connection, in Chromium, and there shouldn't be.\r\n\r\n> And also:\r\n> \r\n> 1. It would simplify JS code, as you now don't have to implement your own buffering logic (for example, you want to connect as soon as possible, but consume potential received messages when something else loads/executes)\r\n\r\nTrue. My suggestion would miss early `open`/`close`/`error`/`message` events.\r\n\r\nWe *could* maybe always buffer events until there's an event listener, similar to workers. (If it's web compatible.)\r\n\r\n> 2. It would give us feature parity for websocket requests, and not have them be something special in terms of resource hints. So this proposal is in my opinion a decrease in complexity.\r\n\r\nYou can't do resource hints for everything already even ignoring WebSockets, e.g. you can't preload a form submission.\r\n\r\nI don't know if there are security implications of preloading a WebSocket connection, but there could be.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
